A||||14||William Federle||Cable operated clutch||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||05-28-2000||12:46 PM||||I saw in the thread concerning electronic techs or engineers an interest in alternate means of releasing/engaging the Fiero clutch.<BR>Has anyone considered using a bowden cable (I think that's the correct spelling) like they do on motorcycles? I guess it might present a whole new set of problems - stretching cables for instance.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||05-28-2000||12:58 PM||||Why try to re invent thee wheel. The fluid system works just fine when maintaines properly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||05-28-2000||02:01 PM||||Given the number of posts on this and other lists concerning clutch problems and diagnosing them, I'd say that this particular wheel leaves something to be desired. It works ok in my car too, but there are a lot of things that can go wrong with it and the symptoms for each aren't very specific - I mean symptoms for one malfunction - leaking seal in master cylinder, can also be the symptom of another - leaking cylinder in slave that lets air in, or problems with the disengaging mechinism in the clutch assem. itself.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||05-28-2000||04:26 PM||||I would be all over that William. <P>====&gt;My opinion, for what it's worth...cables, fewer moving parts and only one system. Good quality cable could easily be purchased, I should think, for the collective dollars of a master and slave cyl. assy's.<P>I don't dissagree with Tom necessarily; For some Fiero owners, there have been very few problems with the clutch hydraulics. Maybe the rest don't know what WE are doing but the ubiquitous clutch problems thread tends to lead me into a numbers thing...most do have problems.<P>I think the open ended design of the slave with the rubber boot is the weak link. I've often wondered if there is a better designed closed slave out here somewhere???<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||05-28-2000||04:28 PM||||Same as most common brake problems. should we start building manual cable brake actuators||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||05-28-2000||04:37 PM||||<BR>Isn't it interesting then Tom, that what we often refer to as the <I><B>emergency</B></I> brake IS cable actuated?<P>Food for thought... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ozzy||05-28-2000||06:21 PM||||Or inversely, a hydraulic throttle link! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I don't know...As I said Tom, I go with the numbers as a rule. Not my intention to start a conflict at all. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> We've all seen enough of that around here recently.<P>respectfully, Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||05-28-2000||10:54 PM||||As both of my Fieros are autos, I don't really have an opinion on the cable operated clutch, but...  What about a wire operated (like in airplanes, just a wire sending signals, no cable) throttle?  Not that we have any serious problems with the throttle cables.  Just a thought.  Or what about a true fly-by-wire system?  Joystick controlled steering (they're doing it already on some Saab or Volvo prototypes).  That would be cool.  (Can you tell I love futuristic, sci-fi, etc stuff? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000008||TOM||05-28-2000||11:21 PM||||sorry for the negs. But Yes the e brake is cable and in not used to the extent that the regular brakes are. I am not saying that the system is perfect. Quite obviously it is not. But I am used to dealing with the brakes on vw bugs and let me tell you they suck. and one 68 alfa I worked on had a cable operated actuator that had a hydralic cylinder to pull the cable. that was crapola too. we swapped in a later rear end to get rid of all that mess. I also deal with aircraft hydralics so a simple clutch is no biggie.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||05-28-2000||11:25 PM||||BMW has had "drive by wire" throttle for years. Vettes have it too. Computer will automatically cut back the throttle to control wheel spin. GM had a twin engine Citation back in the 80's that used the second engine on a demand basis and was able to "dynamically" (on the run) alter throttle between the two engines to compensate for traction and handling. They actually admitted borrowing the technology from BMW- they have a caprice classic with the BMW engine and management system in it for "evaluation purposes". All this is in the storage barn with the 4 wheel steer Camaro, the rear drive Lumina, the v12 Caddy engine from the 60's and other lost toys.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||05-29-2000||03:53 AM||||Let's see...How about a quiet little midnight run to this storage barn?  Anybody with me?||||reg||1
Z||000011||HF6582||05-29-2000||04:34 PM||||I have considered a cable operated clutch for the Fiero, and even shetched a few designs of it.<P>Just haven't designed to self adjuster for it yet.<P>I probably will just stick with the system on the car.<P>Scott<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||05-29-2000||09:41 PM||||Okay. My two cents...<BR>I have had a Chevy Monza and an Olds Starfire (Monza clone) with cable clutches. Also had an 83 Chevy S-10 with a cable clutch. Had problems with all three of them. Up to, and including, one of the firewalls (the Starfire) bending under the stress.<BR>Have a 92 Sonoma and had an 88 Firebird Formula. Both have hydraulic clutches.<BR>Number of problems with the hydraulics? Exactly zero. They never have to be adjusted. The fluid just takes up the slack. All you have to do is make sure the master stays full. And, the pedal pressure remains fairly constant throughout the life of the clutch. <BR>I understand that many folks have trouble with their Fiero clutches, but I wonder what the percentage of the total is. Just by the nature of the forum, all we ever hear about are the broken ones. (No, this is not a complaint. Sharing repair tips and experiences is what the forum is all about.) But I wonder... What about the other members who have clutches that work perfectly? Anybody? <P>Or is the sunburn just getting to me?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ozzy||05-29-2000||10:41 PM||||Raydar's Radar is tuned in pretty good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>If I had to guess of the 1000 or so members I would say 10% or thereabouts...<P><BR>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||05-30-2000||01:33 AM||||Yes, if you could find one long enough a Fiero could be done with a cable clutch. You'd have to work up brackets and a peddle but it could be done.<P>Clutch cables commonly last 75-100,000 miles or more. Especially the newer type cables. In nearly 200,000 miles on my Pinto I replaced the clutch cable exactly once. It was starting to bind.<P>I've only ever seen one cable actually break. It was a mustang II that was improperly worked on after an engine swap.<P>oop I take that back..... my girl friend wore the end button off the one in her old Chevette. It was around 80-90,000 on it.||||reg||1
